## Security Decisions

### Why bcrypt (not Argon2)?
bcrypt is widely understood, has stable library support across all our target languages, and cost-12 provides adequate hardening. Argon2id is theoretically superior but adds an external C dependency on some platforms. This decision is revisitable if requirements change.

### Why not OAuth-only (passwordless)?
OAuth requires users to have an identity provider account. Our target users may be in orgs that don't use Google/GitHub SSO. Username + password remains the lowest common denominator. OAuth can be layered on top later.

### Why inline worker (not separate process)?
Reduces operational complexity for v1. One process to monitor, one crash to handle. When throughput requires it, the worker can be split into a separate process — the Redis Streams architecture already supports it; it's a deployment-time decision, not a code change.

